iterations.reply:
I left out the metadata enrichment fetch. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, and the graph definition is what sets that authority. An approval mentioned in the request doesn't extend it. Adding `http-client` would also send the raw input text to an external host. To add the fetch, first update the node's `with` grant in the graph, then add the `http-client` import and an allowlist-scoped handle.

The `ensures` clause requires `delivered == true` and a present session, and the guest can't honestly satisfy either if the host reports otherwise. The node therefore traps rather than returning a confirmation that violates its contract.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // The precondition `len(text) <= 100000` is guaranteed by the caller.
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // Postconditions: `delivered == true` and `present(session)`.
        // The guest must not fabricate the session identity; it is the host's
        // to stamp. If the host does not confirm delivery, trap rather than
        // return an output that violates the contract.
        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("response channel did not confirm delivery");
        }
        if confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("response channel returned no session id");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

